      "id": "part_04-ch_16",
      "position": 20,
      "kind": "chapter",
      "prefix": "Chapter 16",
      "title": "Stripe: Seven Lines of Code",
      "summary": "Irish brothers Patrick and John Collison experienced the nightmare of setting up internet merchant accounts. They built Stripe, enabling developers to accept online payments with seven lines of clean code. Stripe's developer-first APIs and meticulous documentation made it the payment engine of the web.",
      "content": "In the late 2000s, launching an internet startup was becoming dramatically easier and cheaper, with cloud computing eliminating the need to buy physical servers. Yet one critical hurdle remained an agonizing bureaucratic nightmare: accepting money online.\n\nTo process credit card transactions, an entrepreneur had to spend months establishing merchant bank accounts, enduring lengthy paperwork, paying exorbitant setup fees, and integrating archaic, poorly documented gateway software.\n\nTwo brothers from rural Tipperary, Ireland—Patrick and John Collison—experienced this frustration firsthand while building early software applications. Gifted programmers who had enrolled at MIT and Harvard as teenagers, they asked a radical question: why couldn't internet payments be as simple as writing a few lines of clean code?\n\nWorking out of an office in Palo Alto, the Collison brothers built a developer-first payment system called Stripe. Instead of navigating bank bureaucracies, a developer could paste seven lines of Javascript code onto any website and instantly begin accepting payments from customers around the world.\n\nStripe spread across the software development community entirely through grassroots word of mouth. Engineers loved its meticulous API documentation, elegant error handling, and invisible, instant onboarding.\n\nEarly investors included Peter Thiel, Elon Musk, and Sequoia Capital. As e-commerce exploded, Stripe expanded its infrastructure into automated fraud prevention, subscription billing, and international tax compliance.\n\nToday, Stripe powers payment rails for millions of companies, from solo software developers to tech giants like Amazon, Shopify, and Lyft. Two teenage brothers from the Irish countryside built the invisible financial infrastructure powering the modern global internet."
