      "title": "SpaceX: Reaching for the Stars",
      "summary": "Elon Musk founded SpaceX in 2002 to slash launch costs through reusable rocket engineering. After three catastrophic failures on Kwajalein Island, Falcon 1 reached orbit on its fourth flight in 2008. SpaceX mastered vertical booster landings on land and sea, restoring American space leadership.",
      "content": "In 2001, after selling PayPal to eBay, Elon Musk traveled to Moscow with a bold dream: to buy decommissioned Russian intercontinental ballistic missiles and launch a small greenhouse to Mars to revive public excitement about space exploration. Russian aerospace officials mocked him, demanding exorbitant sums.\n\nFlying home, Musk calculated the raw material cost of an aerospace rocket: aluminum, titanium, copper, and rocket fuel. He discovered that the raw physical elements accounted for only two percent of the total price of a rocket launch.\n\nThe aerospace industry was paralyzed by bloated government contractors, bureaucratic cost-plus contracts, and single-use expendable rockets that were discarded into the ocean after every flight. Space flight would never become affordable until rockets were built like commercial airplanes: fully reusable.\n\nIn 2002, Musk founded Space Exploration Technologies (SpaceX) in an empty warehouse in El Segundo, California. Setting up a remote launch pad on the tiny Pacific atoll of Kwajalein Island, a team of young engineers worked around the clock to build the Falcon 1 rocket.\n\nDisaster struck repeatedly. The first rocket caught fire and crashed in 2006. The second failed to reach orbit in 2007. The third collided with its own first stage during separation in 2008. SpaceX was down to its last dollars; a fourth failure would mean complete bankruptcy.\n\nIn September 2008, Falcon 1 made its fourth attempt, roaring flawlessly into orbit to become the first privately developed liquid-fuel rocket to reach space. NASA awarded SpaceX a crucial commercial cargo contract to resupply the International Space Station.\n\nSpaceX proceeded to develop the Falcon 9 and accomplished the holy grail of rocket engineering: landing massive orbital booster rockets vertically on sea barges and concrete pads. By drastically slashing launch costs, SpaceX restored American space dominance and opened a new era of interplanetary exploration."
